I can see the vulnerability. In `load_protocols_file_fd`, there's a heap-buffer-overflow READ 2. Let me analyze the code carefully:

```c
while(1) {
    char *line = buffer;
    int line_len = buffer_len;

    while(((line = fgets(line, line_len, fd)) != NULL)
          && strlen(line) > 0
          && (line[strlen(line) - 1] != '\n')) {
      i = strlen(line);
      old_buffer = buffer;
      old_buffer_len = buffer_len;
      buffer_len += chunk_len;

      buffer = ndpi_realloc(old_buffer, old_buffer_len, buffer_len);
      ...
      line = &buffer[i];
      line_len = chunk_len;
    } /* while */

    if(!line) /* safety check */
      break;

    i = strlen(buffer);
    if((i <= 1) || (buffer[0] == '#'))
      continue;
    else {
      buffer[i - 1] = '\0';
      i--;

      if((i > 0) && (buffer[i-1] == '\r'))
        buffer[i - 1] = '\0';

      if(buffer[0] == '\0')
        continue;
    }
    ...
}
```

The issue: When `fgets` reads a line that fills the buffer completely (no `\n` found), it reallocs and continues. But there's a subtle bug. Let me think about the READ 2 (2-byte read) heap-buffer-overflow.

The key issue is in the inner while loop. When `fgets` returns a line that fills the buffer exactly (i.e., `line_len - 1` chars read, no newline), it reallocs. But the issue is that `fgets` reads at most `line_len - 1` characters. If the line is exactly `line_len - 1` characters long without a newline, then `fgets` returns non-NULL, `strlen(line) > 0`, and `line[strlen(line)-1] != '\n'`, so it reallocs and continues reading.

Wait, but the READ 2 overflow... Let me think about `line[strlen(line) - 1]`. If `fgets` returns an empty string (just `\n`), then `strlen(line) = 1`, and `line[0]` is `\n`, so the condition `line[strlen(line)-1] != '\n'` is false. That's fine.

Actually, let me think about the case where `fgets` reads exactly `line_len - 1` characters. For example, if `line_len = 1024`, `fgets` reads 1023 chars. If those 1023 chars don't include a newline, then `strlen(line) = 1023`, and `line[1022] != '\n'`, so it reallocs. Then `line = &buffer[i]` where `i = 1023`, and `line_len = chunk_len = 1024`. So the next `fgets` reads into `buffer[1023]` with size 1024, reading up to 1023 more chars. That seems fine.

Hmm, but wait. Let me reconsider. The issue might be with the `fgets` behavior when the buffer is exactly full. Let me think about the case where the line is exactly `line_len - 1` characters. `fgets` reads `line_len - 1` chars and null-terminates. So `strlen(line) = line_len - 1`. Then `line[line_len - 2]` is the last char. If it's not `\n`, we realloc.

Actually, let me look at this from a different angle. The crash is "Heap-buffer-overflow READ 2". This is a 2-byte read. Let me look at `ndpi_handle_rule` which processes the buffer.

Let me look at the git log to see if there's a fix for this.

